event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":881,"item_id":"fc_01f47fbc4914fd27016adb42053e0481918fee6e115745142f","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_input\":\"prior-candidate.json\",\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/33/type\",\"value\":\"Q-dimension of Q tensor_R N for a finitely generated torsion-free R-module N\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/locals/33/declaration\",\"value\":\"For any finitely generated torsion-free R-module N, define rank_R(N)=dim_Q(Q tensor_R N), where Q=Frac(R); this definition does not presuppose that N is free.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/2/text\",\"value\":\"For each i set J_i={c in C: c e_i is in M_R}. First, M_R is finite free over R: if m_1,...,m_n is the original Z-basis of M, then 1 tensor m_1,...,1 tensor m_n is an R-basis of R tensor_Z M, since the Z-coordinate isomorphism M≅Z^n extends to the explicit isomorphism R tensor_Z M≅R^n. The image E_i,i(M_R)=J_i e_i is finitely generated over R: it is the image of this finite module under the R-linear projection E_i,i. It is nonzero because M_R spans V(lambda) over C and E_i,i is a nonzero projection. Since E_i,j preserves M_R, applying it to c e_j proves J_j subset J_i; reversing i,j proves equality. Denote the common module by J. The coordinate projections give the internal direct sum M_R=⊕_{i=0}^{lambda} J e_i: each vector has its unique e-coordinate expansion, and its i-th coordinate belongs to J_i by the definition of J_i. Thus J is finitely generated (as just shown) and torsion-free, since it is an R-submodule of the field C and R is a domain. We now compute scalar-extension dimension before using any rank-one or ideal conclusion. Let Q=Frac(R), and let ι:Q tensor_R M_R→Q^n be the isomorphism obtained by extending the displayed original-basis coordinate isomorphism R tensor_Z M≅R^n; hence dim_Q(Q tensor_R M_R)=n. Tensor the coordinate direct-sum isomorphism with Q. The explicit compatible isomorphism ⊕_{i=0}^{lambda}(Q tensor_R J)→Q tensor_R M_R sends the i-th summand element q tensor c to q tensor(c e_i); its inverse is induced by the coordinate projections onto J e_i followed by e_i-coordinate extraction. Consequently, writing d=dim_Q(Q tensor_R J), finite because J is finitely generated, dimensions give n=(lambda+1)d=nd. Since n=lambda+1 is a positive integer, d=1. In particular the scalar-extension map μ:Q tensor_R J→C, q tensor c↦qc, is injective: Q tensor_R J is the localization of the R-submodule J⊂C, and an element maps to zero only if some nonzero denominator multiplier annihilates an element of J, which torsion-freeness forbids. Its image is nonzero (J is nonzero) and one-dimensional over Q, so choosing any nonzero image element identifies Q tensor_R J with Q as a Q-vector space. This proves the rank-one identification without assuming J free. Now use μ to regard J as a nonzero finitely generated R-submodule of Q. Clear denominators in a finite generating set to obtain a nonzero finitely generated ideal I of R with I=d_0J for some nonzero d_0 in R. Every nonzero q in Q is p^k u for an integer k and a unit u in R; among a finite set of nonzero generators of I choose the least exponent k. Every generator is in p^k R and one is p^k times a unit, so I=p^k R. Hence J is free of rank one. For a generator a of J, the vectors a e_i form an R-basis of M_R by the direct-sum decomposition.\"}]}}"}

