sm R tensor_Z M≅R^n; hence dim_Q(Q tensor_R M_R)=n. Tensor the coordinate direct-sum isomorphism with Q. The explicit compatible isomorphism ⊕_{i=0}^{lambda}(Q tensor_R J)→Q tensor_R M_R sends the i-th summand element q tensor c to q tensor(c e_i); its inverse is induced by the coordinate projections onto J e_i followed by e_i-coordinate extraction. Consequently, writing d=dim_Q(Q tensor_R J), finite because J is finitely generated, dimensions give n=(lambda+1)d=nd. Since n=lambda+1 is a positive integer, d=1. In particular the scalar-extension map μ:Q tensor_R J→C, q tensor c↦qc, is injective: Q tensor_R J is the localization of the R-submodule J⊂C, and an element maps to zero only if some nonzero denominator multiplier annihilates an element of J, which torsion-freeness forbids. Its image is nonzero (J is nonzero) and one-dimensional over Q, so choosing any nonzero image element identifies Q tensor_R J with Q as a Q-vector space. This proves the rank-one identification without assuming J free. Now use μ to regard J as a nonzero finitely generated R-submodule of Q. Clear denominators in a finite generating set to obtain a nonzero finitely generated ideal I of R with I=d_0J for some nonzero d_0 in R. Every nonzero q in Q is p^k u for an integer k and a unit u in R; among a finite set of nonzero generators of I choose the least exponent k. Every generator is in p^k R and one is p^k times a unit, so I=p^k R. Hence J is free of rank one. For a generator a of J, the vectors a e_i form an R-basis of M_R by the direct-sum decomposition.\"}]}}","status":"completed"}}

